 "runtime_seconds": 200.7,
 "interpretation": {
  "method": "Morris elementary-effects screening (200 trajectories x 4 elementary steps, 1024 evaluations) plus paired Latin-hypercube Sobol (N=1024, M=2, 2048 evaluations) over the 12-dimensional v13 calibration space, evaluated on 12 real corpus cases spanning 13 domains and 5 decision outcomes.",
  "reading_rule": "With a 12-case panel and step-function outputs (decision boundaries, guards), total-order indices saturate near 1. S1 and the Morris mu/sigma are the reportable quantities; ST ~ 1 with S1 ~ 0 means the parameter acts through interactions with the thresholds, not as a main effect.",
  "key_findings": {
   "coverage_gate": "largest first-order effect on mean adequacy (S1 = 0.749) and on abstention/permissiveness (S1 = 1.0): the dominant tuning knob, which is why it was moved from an implicit constant to a declared parameter (I44).",
   "gamma": "second largest (S1 = 0.123 on mean adequacy): the epistemic-penalty weight is the main lever among the penalty terms.",
   "zeta": "measurable effect (Morris mu 0.075 on adequacy, S1 = 0.013) but negative on agreement (kappa -0.0013 at 0.05, -0.0030 at 0.10): rejected as a decision input, kept as a diagnostic.",
   "salience_adaptation_s": "Morris mu 0.0004, S1 ~ 0.03 via interactions only: empirically inert, consistent with kappa <= 0 for s = 0.5/1.0/2.0. s = 0 stays canonical.",
   "oversight_attenuation": "no first-order effect on mean adequacy (mu 0.0) but the dominant driver of hard-veto rate (S1 = 0.701): oversight changes how strongly irreversibility blocks, not the adequacy score.",
   "theta_base_mu_C": "near-zero first-order effects on continuous outputs; they act only through guard thresholds (theta_base Morris mu 0.139 on hard-veto rate)."
  },
  "nonlinearity_note": "Sum of S1 over the 12 parameters is below 1 for every output: the model is dominated by threshold interactions rather than additive parameter effects. This is a property of the decision ladder, and it is why calibration evidence in the paper is reported as paired-bootstrap kappa differences, not as sensitivity coefficients."
 }
